  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nToday - Tuesday: Dry and Warming \n\nA relatively benign forecast is on tap for the rest of the weekend \nand the start of the work week. An upper level trough to our north \nwill move southeast through Ontario with its corresponding surface \nlow dragging a cold front to its southwest. This cold front is \nexpected to move through the Upper Midwest overnight, bringing some \nclouds but no rainfall with it, thanks to the dry low level air mass \nin place across the region. Winds will shift to a more northwesterly \ndirection for tomorrow but high temperatures should remain fairly \ncomparable to today in the low to mid 50s, especially as skies clear \nby late morning. Temperatures for the start of the work week will \ncontinue on a slight upward trend into the upper 50s to low 60s with \nweak ridging in place for Monday and early Tuesday.\n\nWednesday - Thursday: Very Low Rain Chances, Temps Cool Back Down\n\nThis ridging will be short lived, breaking down by midday Tuesday. \nRain chances (only 10-20%) return for Wednesday as a highly \npositively tilted trough axis swings through the Great Lakes region. \nWhile much better chances for precipitation will stay closer to the \nGreat Lakes in the form of lake effect precipitation, some light \nshowers may be possible with the LREF 07.12 maintaining a broad 15-\n20% area of rain Wednesday with its Grand Ensemble (EPS, GEFS, \nGEPS). Any chances of snow or mixed precipitation look to stay to \nour north across far Northern Wisconsin and precipitation looks very \nlight overall given the lack of moisture return ahead of this trough \naxis moving through. \n\nA much cooler airmass is expected across the area by Thursday as the \ntrough axis departs and a surface high pressure to our north starts \nto push colder air into the region. Highs by Thursday will likely \nonly be in the mid to upper 40s, a bit of a departure from the \n60s earlier in the week. \n\nNext Weekend: High Pressure Sets In \n\nThe aforementioned Canadian high pressure is expected to sink south \ninto the Upper Midwest by Friday, dominating much of the weather \npattern across the Central CONUS by Saturday. This will keep \nconditions dry so if we don't see any rain with Wednesday's \nactivity, it will likely be a dry forecast through next Sunday at \nleast. Temperatures will be at their coolest Thursday into Friday \nwith some recovery expected by Saturday into the 50s.",
